In C “a *file*” usually refers to an external data stream that your program reads from or writes to—think of it as a persistent storage object outside the process’s memory space.  
C gives you a thin abstraction over this through the **stdio** interface (`<stdio.h>`). Below is a quick primer:

| Concept | What it means / why it matters |
|---------|--------------------------------|
| `FILE` | An opaque structure representing an open file descriptor. You never manipulate its internals directly; instead you pass pointers to it (`FILE *`) into the stdio API. |
| Opening a file | Use `fopen(const char *pathname, const char *mode)`.<br>Mode strings:<br>- `"r"` – read text<br>- `"w"` – write text (truncate)<br>- `"a"` – append text<br>- `"rb/wb/ab"` – same but binary (no translation of line endings) |
| Closing | Call `fclose(FILE *)`. Always close when finished to flush buffers & release OS resources. |
| Reading | - Text: `int fgetc(FILE*)`, `char * fgets(char*, int, FILE*)`, `size_t fread(void *, size_t, size_t, FILE*)`<br>- Binary: use `fread()` (same call works for both modes). |
| Writing | - Text: `int fputc(int, FILE*)`, `int fprintf(FILE*, const char*, …)`, `int fputs(const char*, FILE*)`<br>- Binary: `size_t fwrite(const void *, size_t, size_t, FILE*)` |
| Seeking | `long fseek(FILE*, long offset, int whence);` <br>Use `ftell(FILE*)` to get current position. Useful for random‑access files. |
| Error handling | After each operation check return values (`EOF`, zero bytes read/write, `feof()`, `ferror()`). |
| Buffering | By default streams are fully buffered (writes accumulate until buffer full or flushed). Use `setvbuf()` if you need custom buffering behavior. |

### Example: copy one file to another

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#define BUF_SIZE 4096

int main(void)
{
    FILE *src = fopen("source.bin","rb");
    FILE *dst = fopen("dest.bin","wb");

    if (!src || !dst) {
        perror("fopen");   /* prints error description */
        return 1;
    }

    unsigned char buf[BUF_SIZE];
    size_t n;
    while ((n = fread(buf, 1, sizeof buf, src)) > 0) {
        if (fwrite(buf, 1, n, dst) != n) {      /* check all written */
            perror("fwrite");
            break;
        }
    }

    if (ferror(src))
        perror("fread");

    fclose(src);
    fclose(dst);

    return 0;
}
```

---

#### Quick FAQ

| Question | Answer |
|----------|--------|
| Can I mix text/binary modes? | Yes, just choose the appropriate mode at opening. Mixing within the same stream isn’t allowed. |
| How do I know whether my platform uses CRLF vs LF? | In text mode, the runtime automatically translates newline conventions. If you want raw bytes, always use binary mode (`"rb"/"wb"`). |
| Is there a way to lock a file so others can't modify it concurrently? | Standard C doesn’t provide locking primitives. On POSIX you’d use `flock(2)` or advisory locks via `<fcntl.h>`. On Windows use `LockFileEx`. |
| Why does `fprintf(stdout,"…")` sometimes not appear immediately? | stdout is typically line-buffered when connected to a terminal, otherwise fully buffered. Flush explicitly with `fflush(stdout)` or set unbuffered mode with `setvbuf(stdout,NULL,_IONBF,0)`. |

If you had something else in mind—say working with low‑level file descriptors (`open()/read()/write()`), manipulating directories, or dealing with special device files—just let me know!
